 ],
 "literal": "Now thinking, pity it becomes! For the well-made doll of dense sand, adorning with plucked flowers, / with the girls who play in the cool pond, clasping hands, / whenever they embrace, embracing; whenever they swing, swinging; / with the playmates who know no secrets and have no deceit, / climbing the high branch of the marutam tree that bends near the water-entry, / on the bending branch near the water, climbing, with great splendour, / while those on the bank are amazed, and the inside of the waves splashes, / jumping with a 'tutum' sound into the deep pool of long water, / diving and taking sand, that untaught youth! / It is indeed to be pitied! Where is it now, I wonder? / Leaning on a heavy staff with a metal ring at the top, trembling, / coughing in between, with a few crowded words, / to us indeed, who have become very old men!",
 "translation": "Now, when I think of it, it brings deep sorrow. In those days, we clasped hands with girls who bathed in the cool pond, weaving plucked flowers to adorn dolls made of dense sand. Whenever they embraced, we embraced; whenever they swung, we swung, playing with those artless companions who knew no secrets or deceit. We climbed the high branches of the marutam tree that bent low over the bathing ghat, and from those branches near the water, we leaped with a loud 'tutum' splash into the deep pool, making the waves scatter and the onlookers on the bank marvel. We would dive deep and bring up handfuls of sand from the bottom—oh, that untaught, carefree youth! How pitiable it is! Where has it gone now? For us, who now lean on a heavy staff with a metal ring at its head, trembling, coughing in between, speaking only a few crowded words, having become very old men.",
 "modern": "இப்போது நினைத்துப் பார்த்தால் மிகவும் வருத்தமாக இருக்கிறது. அடர்ந்த மணலால் செய்யப்பட்ட பொம்மைகளுக்குப் பறித்த பூக்களைச் சூட்டி, குளிர்ந்த குளத்தில் விளையாடும் பெண்களோடு கைகோர்த்து, அவர்கள் தழுவும்போது தழுவியும், அவர்கள் ஊசலாடும்போது ஊசலாடியும், இரகசியங்கள் அறியாத, கள்ளமில்லாத தோழர்களோடு கூடி விளையாடினோம். நீர்நிலையின் அருகே தாழ்ந்திருந்த பெரிய கிளைகளைக் கொண்ட மருத மரத்தின் மீது ஏறி, கரையில் இருப்பவர்கள் வியக்கும்படியும், அலைகள் சிதறும்படியும், ஆழமான நீரில் 'துடும்' என்று குதித்து, மூழ்கி மணலை அள்ளி விளையாடிய அந்த அறியாத இளமைப் பருவம் எவ்வளவு இரங்கத்தக்கது! அது இப்போது எங்கே இருக்கிறது? பூண் மாட்டிய பெரிய தடியை ஊன்றி, நடுங்கி, இருமலுக்கு இடையே சில சொற்களை மட்டுமே பேசும் முதியவர்களாகிய நமக்கு அந்த இளமை எங்கே போயிற்று?",
 "explanation": "The poet, Toṭittalai Viḻuttaṇṭiṉār, is speaking to himself or to onlookers in his old age, lamenting the loss of his youth. He contrasts his current state of physical decay with the vibrant, carefree days of his childhood. In his youth, he played with girls, swam in deep pools, and dived to the bottom to bring up sand, showing off his strength and agility. Now, he is a frail old man who must lean on a metal-ringed staff, trembling and coughing, barely able to speak a few words. The poem beautifully captures the universal human experience of aging and the poignant memory of lost youth. The name of the poet itself is derived from the phrase 'toṭittalai viḻuttaṇṭu' (heavy staff with a metal ring) used in this poem.",
